  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Age Intervals, Birth Spacing, and Special Comparisons",
    "description": "Reason about age gaps, spaced births, and comparisons that relate how much younger one person is to another gap.",
    "teachingApproach": "Use short, connected phone-sized teaching screens with concrete ages, labelled dates, separate calculation lines, a reason for each step, and an original-clue check. Place a fresh matching practice question after each worked example; wrong answers receive a worked correction and parallel practice.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 27,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 8, Problems on Ages. Approved scope: Teach fixed age intervals, children born at regular intervals, consecutive or equally spaced ages, and comparisons such as one person's age gap from an older person matching another gap from a younger person. Translate an interval of k years into a fixed difference; represent n children spaced k years apart with a first age plus successive multiples of k; and use symmetry or a midpoint only after deriving it from the equality of gaps. Include time shifts for a group total and distinguish the next child from the next age in a listed sequence. Learners should draw or tabulate the ages, preserve the interval, form the needed equation, and verify every gap. Avoid assumptions about exact birthdays unless the problem supplies them."
  },
  "startNodeId": "start",
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Let's use age gaps to work out a family's ages.\n\nWhen children are born at equal intervals, count the spaces between them. Four children have three spaces, not four.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Picture four siblings from youngest to oldest, each three years apart.\n\nChild 1 → Child 2 → Child 3 → Child 4\n\nThere are three arrows, so the first and last are 3 × 3 = 9 years apart.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1_step"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1_step": {
      "id": "teach_1_step",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the youngest is 7, the ages are:\nYoungest: 7\nNext: 10\nNext: 13\nOldest: 16.\n\nCheck each neighboring gap: 3 years. Count the spaces before multiplying the interval.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
